	"nodes":[
		{"id":"04c5eaae6af61a18","type":"group","x":-320,"y":1000,"width":680,"height":960,"color":"5","label":"Data transformation"},
		{"id":"3d124fc6eb953074","type":"group","x":753,"y":-560,"width":687,"height":840,"color":"2","label":"Configuration & Context"},
		{"id":"ccd7d91bb8cb7313","type":"group","x":720,"y":720,"width":320,"height":416,"color":"1","label":"Exception Handling"},
		{"id":"3c1cde58f4eaff67","type":"text","text":"# Servlet Filter Chain \ne.g. Security, Logging\n\nServlet-level components (part of Java EE, not specific to Spring). They can intercept and modify **requests and responses** before they even reach the Spring dispatcher. Good for logging, compression, security checks (e.g., `CorsFilter`).\n\nFilters are the first line of defense. They are part of the Java Servlet specification and operate outside of Spring's `DispatcherServlet`. They are used for low-level tasks that apply to all web requests, like logging, authentication, character encoding, or security checks. A filter can even block a request from proceeding further.","x":-222,"y":-761,"width":417,"height":501},
		{"id":"02a0b5eabce74709","type":"text","text":"# Dispatcher Servlet\nOnce a request passes through the filters, it is handed to Spring's `DispatcherServlet`. This is the single, central servlet that orchestrates the rest of the request lifecycle within the Spring framework.\n\n[[Dispatcher servlet and Interceptors]]","x":-188,"y":-111,"width":349,"height":291},
		{"id":"89a1a1b180be3c22","type":"text","text":"# HandlerInterceptor  \n## Post-Handle\n[[Dispatcher servlet and Interceptors]]","x":-640,"y":300,"width":380,"height":160},
		{"id":"0cc2a88284fdce83","type":"text","text":"# Properties Files\n\n**`application.properties`/`application.yml`):** These hold key-value pairs for configuration that can change between environments (e.g., dev, prod). They are not just for \"global constants\" but for any externalized configuration. They are injected using **`@Value`** or bound to type-safe **`@ConfigurationProperties`** classes.\n\n[[Properties file]]","x":840,"y":-480,"width":540,"height":280},
		{"id":"d6203f668cedcf08","type":"text","text":"# `@Configuration` Classes\nThis is a specialized `@Component` annotation that marks a class as a **source of bean definitions**. Classes annotated with `@Configuration` contain one or more `@Bean`-annotated methods. These methods tell Spring to create a specific object and register it as a bean. This approach is primarily used for configuring third-party libraries or for setting up beans that require more complex, programmatic creation logic.\n\n[[Configuration Classes]]","x":840,"y":-101,"width":540,"height":321},
		{"id":"210c2535e95b02ce","type":"text","text":"## HTTP Request\n[[HTTP request]]","x":-152,"y":-920,"width":278,"height":100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"250549b63d370671","type":"text","text":"### Entire app","x":960,"y":380,"width":260,"height":60},
		{"id":"40a8bda81dd9958d","type":"text","text":"# Aspects \n@Aspect\nCross-Cutting Concerns","x":-1140,"y":987,"width":341,"height":183,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"85735a036d0cf9ab","type":"text","text":"# HandlerInterceptor  \n## Pre-Handle\n\nThey are a Spring-specific concept that sits between the `DispatcherServlet` and the controller. They are often used for more fine-grained tasks that are aware of the Spring context, like adding attributes to the request, logging controller-specific timing, or performing authorization checks.\n\n[[Dispatcher servlet and Interceptors]]","x":-180,"y":420,"width":390,"height":360},
		{"id":"8db8fbf5cca526d8","type":"text","text":"## HTTP Response\n[[DTO & Response entity]]","x":320,"y":350,"width":240,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c7cb2908ba50311e","type":"text","text":"# Controller  \n@Controller / @RestController\n[[Controllers]]","x":-80,"y":1020,"width":260,"height":160},
		{"id":"fbd80cb39b118e00","type":"text","text":"Global Exception Handler  \n`@ControllerAdvice`\n[[Exception handling]]","x":759,"y":933,"width":237,"height":147},
